import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.DhKOxZu8.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The facilitator","description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton.","frontmatter":{"description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"facilitator.md","filePath":"facilitator.md"}'),r={name:"facilitator.md"};function s(i,e,d,c,h,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="the-facilitator" tabindex="-1">The facilitator <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facilitator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The facilitator&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page zooms in on steps 5 and 6 of <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-66/payment-flow.html">the payment flow</a>, the facilitator&#39;s half. Before any money moves, verify enforces a handful of guarantees — your payment can&#39;t be replayed, tampered with, or under-paid — and settle re-runs all of them before executing on Canton. The facilitator never moves a payer&#39;s funds on its own authority: the only write that moves them is <code>executeAndWaitForTransaction</code> of a <strong>payer-signed</strong> transaction.</p><h2 id="what-verify-guarantees" tabindex="-1">What verify guarantees <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-verify-guarantees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What verify guarantees&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These run in order, with no chain write. The first six are shared code — the same <code>@chainsafe/x402-core</code> verifier the SDK exposes — so a payer can pre-flight its payload against them. The last is the facilitator&#39;s alone, since only it holds the state involved:</p><ol><li><strong>Right scheme and network</strong> — the payload, the requirements, and the network this facilitator is bound to all agree.</li><li><strong>Still valid</strong> — <code>validBefore</code> is still in the future.</li><li><strong>Bound to your exact terms</strong> — the payload&#39;s <code>requirementsHash</code> must match the requirements it is verified against. That hash covers <code>payTo</code>, <code>maxAmountRequired</code>, <code>resource</code> and <code>nonce</code>, so it proves the payload refers to your terms; check 6 proves the signed transfer satisfies them.</li><li><strong>The key belongs to the payer</strong> — the fingerprint derived from the payload&#39;s <code>publicKey</code> must match the <code>::</code> suffix of the payer&#39;s party id, so a caller can&#39;t sign as a party it doesn&#39;t control.</li><li><strong>The signature is valid</strong> — the payer&#39;s key signed the prepared transaction&#39;s hash, and Canton checks that signature against the transaction itself when settle executes it.</li><li><strong>The signed transfer actually pays you.</strong> The prepared transaction is decoded and its sender, receiver, instrument and amount are checked against the requirements, so a valid signature over a divergent transfer is rejected with <code>transfer_mismatch</code> and the field that diverged. Overpaying is allowed; underpaying is not.</li><li><strong>No replay, and you&#39;re an allowed destination</strong> — the nonce has not been used before (the facilitator tracks that), and for an authenticated merchant <code>payTo</code> must be a party that key is allowed to receive at (your destination policy). A destination denial comes back as <code>transfer_mismatch</code> with a <code>destination_not_allowed</code> detail on verify, and as <code>unauthorized</code> on settle.</li></ol><h2 id="what-settlement-does" tabindex="-1">What settlement does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-settlement-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What settlement does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Funds are real Splice <strong>Amulet</strong> (Canton Coin), transferred through the Token Standard&#39;s <code>TransferFactory</code> in one atomic Canton transaction. When your party has a <code>TransferPreapproval</code>, that transfer is direct, so nothing is escrowed or locked up; without one, the Token Standard makes it an offer that completes only once you accept it. What is recorded is the signed transfer&#39;s amount, not the quoted floor, so an overpayment is not understated.</p>',7)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
